  "title": "Roll It Backwards",
  "story": "The director adores the gloomy audition monologue in `scene.txt`, but for the finale she wants it\nperformed bottom to top. \"Trust me,\" she says, \"it's a completely different film backwards.\"",
  "description": "Play the script backwards: print every line of `scene.txt`, beginning with its final line and\nending with its opening one.\n\nEach line itself stays exactly as written (no mirror writing, please).",
  "hints": [
   "Read the name `cat` from right to left. That's the command you want, and it does exactly what its name suggests.",
   "`tac scene.txt`. Careful with `rev`: it reverses the characters inside each line, not the order of the lines."
  ],
  "learn": "`tac` flips the order of the lines; `rev` flips the characters within each line, and mixing the\ntwo up gives you mirror writing. Other ways to rewind: stamp line numbers and sort them high to\nlow (`nl -ba | sort -rn | cut -f2-`), collect the lines in an awk array and loop down from `NR`,\nor use sed's `1!G;h;$!d`, which builds the reversed text in its spare buffer and prints it when\nthe last line arrives.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "tac scene.txt",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "sed '1!G;h;$!d' scene.txt",
    "note": "sed's famous party trick: each new line goes on top of everything saved so far (`G`, `h`), and only the final pile is printed."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "awk '{ line[NR] = $0 } END { for (i = NR; i > 0; i--) print line[i] }' scene.txt",
    "note": "Memorise every line, then deliver them from the last to the first."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "nl -ba scene.txt | sort -rn | cut -f2-",
    "note": "`nl` stamps a number on each line, `sort -rn` puts the biggest number on top, and `cut` peels the numbers off."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "mapfile -t l < scene.txt; for ((i = ${#l[@]} - 1; i >= 0; i--)); do printf '%s\\n' \"${l[i]}\"; done",
    "note": "Load the script into a bash array and walk it backwards by index."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 180,
