  "title": "Zero Padding",
  "story": "The photo archive needs names that sort properly, because `img_10` sorting before `img_2`\nhas already caused one wedding to be shown out of order.",
  "description": "Print the file names `img_001.png` through `img_120.png`, one per line, in order.\n(Just print them; don't create any files.)",
  "hints": [
   "`printf` can pad numbers: `%03d` means \"at least 3 digits, padded with zeros\".",
   "Feed it numbers with `$(seq 1 120)`, or let bash do it: `{001..120}`."
  ],
  "learn": "Zero-padding makes text sorting match numeric order: `img_002` < `img_010` < `img_100`.\n`printf '%03d' 7` prints `007`; printf repeats its format for every extra argument.\n`seq -w` pads automatically, `seq -f` takes a printf-style format, and bash brace\nexpansion like `{001..120}` preserves the zeros you write.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "printf 'img_%03d.png\\n' $(seq 1 120)",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "seq -f 'img_%03g.png' 1 120",
    "note": "seq has a printf-style format built in."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "for i in {001..120}; do echo \"img_$i.png\"; done",
    "note": "Bash brace expansion keeps the leading zeros if you write them."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "seq -w 1 120 | sed 's/.*/img_&.png/'",
    "note": "`seq -w` equalizes widths; sed wraps each number in its costume."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "printf 'img_%s.png\\n' {001..120}",
    "note": "Brace expansion feeding printf. No loop required."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "for ((i = 1; i <= 120; i++)); do printf 'img_%03d.png\\n' \"$i\"; done",
    "note": "A C-style for loop, for the nostalgic."
   }
  ],
