  "dir": "/var/challenges/department_totals",
  "title": "Department Totals",
  "story": "Budget season! Finance needs the total salary of every department, alphabetized,\nbefore the coffee runs out. The coffee is already running out.",
  "description": "`payroll.csv` has a header row and columns `name,department,salary`.\n\nFor each department print `<department> <total salary>` (one space between them),\nsorted alphabetically by department.",
  "hints": [
   "awk arrays can use strings as keys: `t[$2] += $3` adds each salary to its department's total.",
   "Print everything in an `END` block with `for (d in t)`, then pipe to `sort` (awk's loop order is random-ish)."
  ],
  "learn": "\"Group by\" in awk is one line: an associative array keyed by the group column accumulates values,\nand an `END` block prints the results. awk doesn't promise any order in `for (k in arr)`, so pipe\nthe output through `sort`. If the input is already sorted by the group key, a streaming version\nthat prints whenever the key changes works too.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "awk -F, 'NR > 1 { t[$2] += $3 } END { for (d in t) print d, t[d] }' payroll.csv | sort",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "tail -n +2 payroll.csv | sort -t, -k2,2 | awk -F, '$2 != d { if (d != \"\") print d, s; d = $2; s = 0 } { s += $3 } END { print d, s }'",
    "note": "Sort by department first, then sum streaming groups. Uses almost no memory."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "for d in $(tail -n +2 payroll.csv | cut -d, -f2 | sort -u); do echo \"$d $(grep \",$d,\" payroll.csv | cut -d, -f3 | paste -sd+ | bc)\"; done",
    "note": "One department at a time: grep its rows, add up the salaries with paste and bc."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "awk -F, 'NR > 1 { sum[$2] += $3 } END { for (k in sum) printf \"%s %d\\n\", k, sum[k] }' payroll.csv | sort -k1,1",
    "note": "printf for full control over the output format."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 210,
  "tags": [
   "awk",
   "arrays",
   "group-by",
   "csv"
  ],
