  "story": "Marketing wants to know which email providers dominate the newsletter list.\nHalf the list typed their domain in ALL CAPS because they were excited.",
  "description": "`subscribers.txt` has one email address per line. Count subscribers per domain (the part\nafter `@`), treating domains **case-insensitively** (report them in lowercase).\n\nPrint `<count> <domain>` lines sorted by count (highest first); domains with the same count\nare sorted alphabetically (A to Z).",
  "hints": [
   "Domain = everything after the `@` (`cut -d@ -f2`), lowercased with `tr`.",
   "Count with `sort | uniq -c`. For the tie-break, sort by two keys: `sort -k1,1nr -k2,2`."
  ],
  "learn": "`sort -rn` alone breaks ties by comparing whole lines **in reverse**, so equal counts come out\nZ to A. When order matters, spell out every key: `-k1,1nr` (count, numeric, descending)\nthen `-k2,2` (name, ascending). Normalizing case before counting (`tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'`) is what\nmakes `Gmail.com` and `gmail.com` the same bucket.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "cut -d@ -f2 subscribers.txt | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' | sort | uniq -c | sort -k1,1nr -k2,2",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "awk -F@ '{ c[tolower($2)]++ } END { for (d in c) print c[d], d }' subscribers.txt | sort -k1,1nr -k2",
    "note": "Count with awk, then the same two-key sort."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "sed 's/.*@//' subscribers.txt | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | sort | uniq -c | sort -k1nr -k2,2",
    "note": "sed deletes everything up to the `@`."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -o '@.*' subscribers.txt | cut -c2- | tr A-Z a-z | sort | uniq -c | sort -k1,1rn -k2,2",
    "note": "grep -o grabs the `@domain` part; cut drops the `@`."
   }
  ],
