  "description": "The herd is grazing in `island/`, one file per cow. Load them all into the cup: move **every**\nfile from `island/` into `cup/`, so the island ends up empty and every cow arrives with her\nname unchanged. (Some cows have two-word names.)",
  "hints": [
   "`mv` moves files: `mv SOURCE... DESTINATION/`. Give it several sources and they all go to the last argument.",
   "A glob can name the whole herd at once: `island/*`. So: `mv island/* cup/`."
  ],
  "learn": "`mv a b c dir/` moves every listed file into `dir/`, so with a glob, `mv island/* cup/`\nrelocates a whole directory's worth of files in one go. The glob turns each name, spaces and\nall, into its own argument, which is why the cows with two-word names arrive in one piece.\n`mv -t dir files...` names the destination first, which is handy with `xargs`. Remember that `*`\nskips hidden files: run `shopt -s dotglob` first if those need to move too.",
