  "title": "Labor XI: Golden Apples",
  "story": "In the garden of the Hesperides grows a tree of golden apples, guarded by Ladon, a dragon who\nkeeps a diary. Legend says every golden apple carries a golden rule of the command line.",
  "description": "The tree's branches are folders inside `tree/`. Each file named `apple` holds a few lines, and\nexactly one of them contains the word `golden`: that's the apple's golden rule. Search **only\nthe files named `apple`** (pears and diaries don't count) and print every line that contains\n`golden`, without file names in front. Order doesn't matter.",
  "hints": [
   "Find the apples first: `find . -name apple` lists every file with exactly that name.",
   "`find ... -exec COMMAND {} \\;` runs a command on every file it finds, with `{}` replaced by the file's path.",
   "`find . -name apple -exec grep golden {} \\;`"
  ],
  "learn": "`-exec` turns `find` into a conveyor belt: for each file it finds, it runs your command with\n`{}` swapped for that file's path, and `\\;` marks where the command ends (the backslash keeps\nbash from grabbing the `;` itself). Ending with `+` instead hands many files to a single\ncommand, which is faster. `grep` on one file prints just the matching\nlines; on several it puts `file:` in front, unless you add `-h`. A plain `grep -r` would also\nhave caught the pear and the dragon's diary, so filtering by name first is what keeps them out.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "find . -name apple -exec grep golden {} \\;",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "find tree -name apple -exec grep -h golden {} +",
    "note": "With `+`, one grep gets the whole basket at once; `-h` drops the file names."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -h golden tree/*/apple",
    "note": "Every apple hangs exactly one branch deep, so a glob can reach them all."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "find . -type f -name apple | xargs grep -h golden",
    "note": "Pick the apples, then let xargs carry them over to grep."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -rh --include=apple golden .",
    "note": "Recursive grep, limited to files called `apple`. The pear never stood a chance."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "for a in tree/*/apple; do grep golden \"$a\"; done",
    "note": "Walk along the branches and taste each apple in turn."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 180,
  "tags": [
   "find",
   "grep",
   "exec"
  ],
