  "dir": "/var/challenges/gremlin_bearings",
  "title": "Get Your Bearings",
  "story": "A gremlin got into the arcade's computer and is snacking its way through `/bin` and `/usr/bin`.\n`ls`, `cat` and friends are already gone. Only the commands baked right into bash survived.",
  "description": "Every program on disk has been eaten, so for this whole side quest you'll have to make do with\n**bash builtins**: commands that live inside the shell itself.\n\nStep one is getting your bearings: show the full path of the directory your shell is sitting\nin right now.",
  "hints": [
   "The programs in `/bin` are gone, but some commands were never files in the first place. Ask bash about one: `type pwd`.",
   "`pwd` survived. Bash also stores the answer in the variable `$PWD`."
  ],
  "learn": "A builtin is a command that bash carries around inside itself instead of loading it from disk,\nso a gremlin with an appetite for `/bin` can't get its teeth into it. The survivors include\n`pwd`, `echo`, `printf`, `cd`, `read`, `test`, `kill` and `type`. Ask `type pwd` and bash\nreplies *pwd is a shell builtin*; on a healthy machine, `type ls` would name a file such as\n`/usr/bin/ls` instead. Your location is also stored in the variable `$PWD` (the previous one in\n`$OLDPWD`), so `echo \"$PWD\"` doesn't even need a command.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "pwd",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "echo \"$PWD\"",
    "note": "Bash keeps your location in a variable, so no command has to run at all."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "printf '%s\\n' \"$PWD\"",
    "note": "`printf` also lives inside bash, and unlike echo it never mistakes your text for options."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "dirs",
    "note": "The directory stack always starts with the place you're standing."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "echo \"$(pwd)\"",
    "note": "A command substitution of a builtin needs no program either."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 180,
  "tags": [
   "builtins",
   "pwd"
  ],
