  "dir": "/var/challenges/trick_or_treat",
  "title": "Trick or Treat",
  "story": "Jack O'Latency guards the porch of Grimwood Manor. He does light up for every visitor...\neventually. Luckily the doorbell camera wrote everything down in `porch.log`.",
  "description": "Print every line of `porch.log` that contains the word `treat` in any capitalisation\n(`treat`, `Treat`, `TREAT`, ...), in the original order.\n\nIt has to be the word on its own, not glued to other letters: `retreat`, `treaty` and\n`treatments` don't count (but `Trick-or-treat` does).",
  "hints": [
   "`grep` finds lines, but it is picky about capital letters. One flag makes it **i**gnore case.",
   "Another flag only accepts whole **w**ords, so `retreat` stops sneaking in.",
   "Flags can share a dash: `grep -iw treat porch.log`."
  ],
  "learn": "`grep -i` ignores case, and `-w` only accepts a match that isn't glued to other letters,\ndigits or underscores: `Trick-or-treat` counts, `retreat` doesn't. Flags can be bundled, so\n`-iw` is the same as `-i -w`. Inside a GNU regex you can mark word edges yourself with `\\b`,\nor with `\\<` (start of a word) and `\\>` (end of a word).",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "grep -iw treat porch.log",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -i '\\btreat\\b' porch.log",
    "note": "`\\b` marks a word boundary: the regex way of saying \"on its own\"."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -i '\\<treat\\>' porch.log",
    "note": "`\\<` and `\\>` match the start and the end of a word."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "sed -n '/\\btreat\\b/Ip' porch.log",
    "note": "GNU sed prints (`p`) only the lines whose address matches; `I` makes it case-blind."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "awk 'tolower($0) ~ /(^|[^a-z])treat([^a-z]|$)/' porch.log",
    "note": "Lowercase each line, then insist on a non-letter (or the line's edge) on both sides."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 120,
  "tags": [
   "grep",
   "case-insensitive",
   "whole-words"
  ],
