  "learn": "`sed -n 'Np'` prints only line N: `-n` switches off sed's automatic printing and `p` prints the\naddressed line. `awk 'NR == 13'` does the same with awk's line counter, and\n`head -n 13 | tail -n 1` keeps the last of the first 13 lines. Ranges work too:\n`sed -n '10,15p'`. Careful with `nl`: it skips blank lines when numbering, `cat -n` doesn't.",
