  "learn": "`sed 's/pattern/replacement/g'` rewrites every line and prints the result; the file itself\nstays untouched unless you add `-i`. Without the `g` flag only the first match on each line\nis replaced. GNU sed understands `\\b` (a word boundary) and `\\<` / `\\>` (start / end of a\nword), so `\\btoad\\b` won't bite into `toadstool`. Several commands can share one sed,\nseparated by `;` or given as separate `-e` options.",
