  "dir": "/var/challenges/potluck_signup",
  "title": "Potluck Signup",
  "story": "The potluck sign-up sheet went around the barn twice and came back covered in gravy.\nSir Gobbles only needs the menu for the chalkboard. Who brought what can wait.",
  "description": "Each line of `potluck.txt` is a guest's name, a colon, **one space**, and the dish they are\nbringing, like `Aunt Hazel: green bean casserole`. Some names contain spaces.\n\nPrint only the **dishes**, one per line, in the same order as the file (if two guests bring\nthe same dish, it appears twice). Don't keep the space that followed the colon.",
  "hints": [
   "`cut` chops each line at a delimiter of your choice: `-d:` splits at colons and `-f2` keeps the second piece.",
   "`cut -d: -f2` leaves a space in front of every dish. Shave it off with `| cut -c2-`, or split on colon-space in one go with `awk -F': '`."
  ],
  "learn": "`cut -d<char> -f<N>` splits every line at a single delimiter character and keeps field N\n(`-f2-` keeps field 2 and everything after it); `cut -c<N>-` cuts by character position instead.\ncut can't split on two characters, but `awk -F': '` can, and `sed 's/^[^:]*: //'` simply deletes\nthe prefix. Splitting on spaces (`cut -d' '`) would fail here: `Aunt Hazel` has one too.",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "cmd": "cut -d: -f2 potluck.txt | cut -c2-",
    "note": "Reference solution"
   },
   {
    "cmd": "sed 's/^[^:]*: //' potluck.txt",
    "note": "Delete everything up to and including the colon-space. The menu remains."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "awk -F': ' '{ print $2 }' potluck.txt",
    "note": "awk accepts a multi-character field separator: split on colon-space and print the right half."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "cut -d: -f2 potluck.txt | sed 's/^ //'",
    "note": "cut first, then let sed trim the leading space."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "while IFS=: read -r name dish; do echo \"${dish# }\"; done < potluck.txt",
    "note": "bash splits each line at the colon by itself; `${dish# }` removes one leading space."
   },
   {
    "cmd": "grep -o ': .*' potluck.txt | cut -c3-",
    "note": "Grab the colon and everything after it, then drop the first two characters."
   }
  ],
  "timeLimit": 120,
